\documentclass[a4paper]{article}
%\VignetteIndexEntry{Greatest Common Divisor}
%\VignettePackage{numTheory}

\title{Greatest Common Divisor using gcd_cpp}
\author{Siddhesh Akole}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

This vignette demonstrates the \code{gcd_cpp} function from the \pkg{numTheory} package.
The function computes the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers efficiently
using a C++ backend. The GCD is the largest integer that divides both numbers without
leaving a remainder.

\section{Inputs}
The function takes two integer inputs:
- \code{a}: the first integer.  
- \code{b}: the second integer.  
The output is a single integer representing the greatest common divisor of \code{a} and \code{b}.

\section{Basic Example}
Here we compute the GCD of two numbers:

<<echo=TRUE, results=TRUE>>=
library(numTheory)

# Compute gcd of 48 and 18
gcd_cpp(48, 18)
@

\section{Properties}
The GCD has several useful properties:
- If one number divides the other, the GCD is the smaller number.  
- If the numbers are coprime (no common factors), the GCD is 1.  

<<echo=TRUE, results=TRUE>>=
# Coprime example
gcd_cpp(17, 29)

# Divisible example
gcd_cpp(12, 36)
@

\section{Constraints}
In R, exact integer numerical range is: \code{.Machine\$integer.max} to  \code{- .Machine\$integer.max}.  
For doubles, the numerical range is: \code{.Machine\$double.xmax}  to \code{.Machine\$double.xmin}.

\section{Complexity}
The \code{gcd_cpp} function uses the Euclidean algorithm, which runs in 
\(\mathcal{O}(\log \min(a,b))\) time.

\section{Summary}
The \code{gcd_cpp} function provides a fast and reliable way to compute the greatest
common divisor, making it useful for number theory, algorithms, and cryptographic
applications.

\end{document}
